{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786210149,"nonce":"wxnfKRTSkqYY7VWnsO1pNI2rAOI=","target":"0646b8b85c865e358e7de03dad00fb7c68026865f2e4ece465ce4d43af174cbe","message":"**What is wrong.** `internal/identity/` has three test files\n(`domain/settings_test.go`, `domain/token_prefix_test.go`,\n`service/settings_test.go`), all written as regressions for specific review\nfindings. The authentication surface itself is untested: `service/login.go`\n(argon2id verify, `DummyVerify` on the unknown-user path), session issue/resolve/\nexpiry and the `DeleteExpired` sweep, PAT mint/verify/revoke with the\nconstant-time prefix compare, the org-blind `ResolveByEmail` `LIMIT 2` behaviour,\nand the `bootstrap` subcommand that refuses to run twice.\n\n**Why it matters.** This is the only thing standing between the internet and every\ntenant's alert history. The security audit read the code and judged it sound, but\nreading is not a gate: `TokenPrefixLen` was a single wrong constant that made\n`POST /api/v1/sources` return 422 forever and left orphan rows\n(`review-security.md` C2), and no test caught it because there were none. A\nregression in session expiry or PAT comparison is invisible until it is exploited.\n\n**Where.** `internal/identity/{domain,service,repository}/`,\n`internal/platform/ratelimit/` (the login limiter), `cmd/oto` bootstrap.\n\n**Done when.** Tests cover: correct/incorrect password, unknown user taking the\nsame path and time, session lifetime and revocation, expired-session resolve\nfailing closed, PAT mint→verify→revoke, a PAT rejected on session-only routes and\nvice versa, ingest tokens rejected on the API, the login rate limiter tripping and\nrecovering, and `bootstrap` refusing a second run.","files":null}]}